--investigationallegation
update
    investigationallegation
set
    med_assessmnts = 'This worker talked with Dr. Mark Langfitt who has concerns about Mason`s behavior
    and Mr.Clopein`s behavior.  Mr. Langfitt is one of the only professionals who has managed to maintain a long term relationship with Mr. Clopein.  Dr. Langfitt said that Mason has many issues and can be scary. He said that Mason is a huge challenge day to day for Mr. Clopein. Dr. Langfitt said that Mason is deep in the Autism Spectrum and has Anxiety and Disruptive Behavior Disorder. There may be other diagnoses as well. Dr. Langfitt said that it is very hard to get Mason on medication and find something that works. Dr. Langfitt said that Mason` s behavior will continue to escalate
    and the situation is volatile.Mason may hurt himself or his dad and could also break out of the house.Dr.Langfitt stated that Mr.Clopein`s issues complicate the situation as he is very difficult. 
This worker has called several mental health providers who have already seen Mason and cannot accommodate his needs such as Crossroads, For All Seasons, and Eastern Shore Psychological Services.  The only ABA therapists who would service Mason are from Ocean Front Counseling, however they are refusing to provide services because Mr. Clopein stated that he would throw them out if they tried to send someone.  
This worker requested medical records from Dr. Langfitt, Anne Arundel Medical Center, University of Maryland, Kennedy Krieger, and Sheppard Pratt.  This worker has not received any records from University of MD or Sheppard Pratt at this time.  
There were several recommendations made by Kennedy Krieger such as genetic testing, audiology testing, Outpatient Neurobehavioral Unit therapy, and ABA therapy.  Anne Arundel also recommends ABA therapy.
This worker contacted several hospitals about inpatient and/or diagnostic hospital stays.  This worker contacted Stanley Benn at Arrow, but they are no longer doing diagnostic stays. St. Vincent`s stated they would only take a child if the State had custody of the child.  There was no other way that this service was possible.  
This worker contacted Terri Parsons at Kennedy Krieger to determine if they were able to help with inpatient and they stated that the only way to do that would be through the Neurobehavioral Unit therapy.  If they did not feel that this was appropriate for Mason they would have to send him to inpatient.  
This worker also contacted Hon Yin Eubanks, the admissions coordinator at Benedictine School, and the only option to get Mason in there was for the State to have custody, or have him attend via the Autism Waiver Registry.',
    collateral_interviews = 'This worker has talked to many other community partners who have experienced difficulty in getting Mr. Clopein to cooperate with them for the benefit of Mason.  Jennifer Malm at DDA was unable to get an application submitted because Mr. Clopein would not sign consents needed for documents that were necessary for the application.  
According to Tara Morse with Mobile Crisis Stabilization Services, Mr. Clopein has refused Mobile Crisis Stabilization Services several times, until the recent FIM. At this time Mobile Crisis Stabilization services are re-evaluating if they are able to serve Mason because of his aggressive behavior towards Ms. Morse.   This worker witnessed Ms. Morse being hit by Mason, on the same day this worker was punched in the forehead and had an open carton of orange juice thrown at this worker.  Ms. Morse reported that at a later visit she had a toy airplane thrown at her head.  Ms. Morse agrees that Mason is a danger to himself and others at all times.
This worker spoke with Stephanie at Ocean Front Counseling, the only ABA provider that has been located thus far.  This worker was told that Mr. Clopein threatened to throw anyone out who came to provide services from their agency.  Ocean Front Counseling informed this worker that they would not be able to provide services to Mason under Mr. Clopein`s care.  
This worker spoke with Justin Turner at the University of Maryland Hospital and was told that  they  were attempting to place Mason at Sheppard Pratt but Sheppard Pratt refused to accept Mason as a patient.  University of Maryland released Mason stating that his behavior was his "base line". They said that Sheppard Pratt would not take Mason and said that Mr. Clopein is known to the Senior Vice President of Sheppard Pratt and he was blocking the placement.
This worker talked to Mason`s special education teacher, Mrs. Elizabeth Stanton, who reported that Mason uses the bathroom at school and does not hit others when he is at school.  The school`s biggest concern was Mason coming and going to school and his apprehension about that.  Mr. Clopein was supposed to have an IEP on 1/4/21, but refused to participate.  With Ms. Stirling Ward coaxing him he finally agreed to have an IEP meeting that he attended on 1/25/21.  Mr. Clopein agreed to psychological and academic testing.  Ms. Ward spoke to this worker and eluded to the fact that having the school pay for a different educational setting is not where they are at this time.',
    expert_assessmnts = 'There have been several hospitalizations that came with recommendations to participate in ABA therapy, get an MRI, have genetic testing done, have audiological testing done, etc.  This worker has collected documentation from Anne Arundel Medical Center, Dr. Langfitt, and Kennedy Krieger.  This documentation is located in the Documents Tab of this case.  Dr. William Cerrato from For All Seasons was prescribing Mason`s psychiatric medications,
    however Mr.Clopein was unable to secure ABA therapy
    and For All Season`s discharged Mason because they required that he receive the ABA therapy to prescribe his psychiatric medications.  This worker has yet to find a psychiatrist who is willing to treat him without therapy.  This worker has talked with Ameila Foxwell from Sweet Bay Magnolia regarding ABA and Julie Vallecillo from Kinera Foundation for recommendations for ABA, psychiatry, and a car seat.  This worker spoke with Audra Cherbonnier at Mid Shore Behavioral Health and Eastern Shore Psychological Services who reported they would not be able to meet Mason`s needs. 
This worker also called Paula Turner and Becky Lepter at Corsica River Mental Health who tried to assist, but after two visits determined Mason`s needs were greater than they could accommodate.'
where
    allegationid = 'e54563a4-b41c-4071-bfc0-fc3d33a052f5'
    and investigationallegationid = '5594ec7d-a60e-4c65-b912-6de9d3dd814a';

--caseclosuresummary
update
    caseclosuresummary
set
    reason = 'Due to Mason`s special needs and Mr. Clopein`s mental health, several services were offered to mitigate the risks and safety concerns for Mr. Clopein and Mason as noted below.  
This worker contacted Learning Together LLC, Sweet Bay Magnolia Academy, Little Red Car, Verbal Beginnings, Applied ABC and Ocean Front Counseling to determine if they were able to provide Applied Behavioral Analysis (ABA) services to Mason and take his insurance.  The only agency identified as both taking Medicaid and providing ABA services in Church Hill was Ocean Front Counseling.  They would not provide services because Mr. Clopein threatened them when they offered services before.  This worker is still pursuing this as it is a needed resource to assist the family with behavior modification.
This worker attempted to refer Mr. Clopein for a psychiatric evaluation, but Mr. Clopein refused.  
This worker contacted Haven Ministries and Cindy Boone with Community Services to assist Mr. Clopein in obtaining resources to pay his rent.  Haven Ministries would not assist, as Mr. Clopein made an arson threat in the past and was charged criminally for it.  Ms. Boone`s decision is pending.
This worker went with Mr. Clopein and Mason to the University of Maryland Hospital in Baltimore because Mason was presenting as a danger to himself and others. Mason spent several days there  but was not able to gain admission to Sheppard Pratt because the Senior Vice President blocked his admission.  He was released because he was reportedly stable, yet his behaviors remain the same.  
This agency offered respite through Crossroads and referred the family for services through Crossroads and Mobile Crisis Stabilization Services (MCSS).  Becky Lepter at Crossroads stated that they did not have services to assist the family and MCSS is providing services but the Tara Morse stated that she may have to close the case due to Mason`s aggressive behaviors towards her.
This worker initiated personal care services provided through Lynn Lamb and the Community First Choice Program and contacted Jennifer Malm with DDA to assist Mr. Clopein with applying for DDA services for Mason.  This worker was told that in the past Mr. Clopein was uncooperative in getting the information needed to complete the DDA application.  This worker is assisting him with the process now.
This worker is in the midst of locating an appropriate car seat to restrain Mason safely during transports.  This agency has agreed to pay for it.  Once this is purchased Mr. Clopein may be able to take Mason to Kennedy Krieger for recommended services there to assist them.
This worker has also called Esther Curtis with Sun Behavioral and Fran Nichols from A.I. Dupont to determine if they have services to assist the family.',
    recommendation = 'This agency recommends that Mr. Clopein and Mason receive In Home Services through the Department and accept any additional services recommended to assist them, including the possibility of inpatient for Mason and services noted above.',
    referralreason = 'This referral was made because several community agencies had concerns about Mason.  Recently Mason was discharged from For All Seasons and was not getting his medications prescribed by a psychiatrist. Mr. Clopein refused services to assist him with Mason like ABA therapy, Mobile Crisis Stabilization Services, and MD Coalition.  This agency is concerned because of the current pandemic and the isolation of the family from supports that would usually be in place for Mason.  Mason has been diagnosed with Autism and has limited ability to communicate.  Mason`s behaviors are nearly unmanageable by Mr. Clopein and there are dangerous behaviors that Mason displays that makes him unsafe to himself and his father, or others that he is around.'
where
    caseclosuresummaryid = '9354c26a-a46b-4c39-9109-5365005532c7'